WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
In a grasshopper embryo, a young leg is growing.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.320
The first axons set out for the central nervous system.

3
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.240
No other axons are ahead of them.

4
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.000
There is nothing to follow.

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.800
Yet kill one guidepost cell before they get there, and they lose their way.

6
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.440
So how does a growth cone know which way to go?

7
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.943
The growth cone does not carry a map.

8
00:00:29.443 --> 00:00:32.623
It reads its surroundings one stretch at a time.

9
00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:38.383
A long route is hard to steer in one go, so it is cut into short legs.

10
00:00:39.123 --> 00:00:41.883
A trail with signposts is easy to follow.

11
00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:44.523
Axons use the same trick.

12
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.703
The first axons through a territory are called pioneers.

13
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:52.183
Along their route sit guidepost cells.

14
00:00:52.943 --> 00:00:57.343
At a guidepost, the growth cone stops, reads, and changes course.

15
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:03.607
The first axons belong to a pair of nerve cells called Ti1.

16
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:10.907
The route has several turns, and at one sits a group of guidepost cells called Cx1.

17
00:01:10.907 --> 00:01:15.107
The growth cones touch them just before they turn.

18
00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:19.627
Bentley and Caudy asked whether the growth cones need them.

19
00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:24.747
They destroyed Cx1 with focused light before the growth cones arrived.

20
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.670
Then the pioneers failed to take the normal route.

21
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.730
They sent branches in abnormal directions.

22
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.730
The conclusion is narrow, and that is its strength.

23
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.610
At this turn, in this preparation, that cell is needed.

24
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.790
The experiment does not say whether the cell provides adhesion or a signal.

25
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.320
Later work found that the full route is built from several legs.

26
00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.880
They are read partly from guideposts, and partly from boundaries in the surrounding

27
00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.820
tissue. A guidepost is one important cue among several.

28
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.200
Later axons, called followers, often grow along the pioneers.

29
00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:13.813
So a growth cone does not steer a whole route at once.

30
00:02:14.353 --> 00:02:19.873
It reads its surroundings one stretch at a time, and some of what it reads is other

31
00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:26.233
cells. In the grasshopper leg, remove one guidepost, and the pioneers lose their

32
00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:27.613
normal route at that turn.

33
00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:32.613
Keep this rule: a long route is steered in short legs.
